The unchanged output is the baseline-invariance theorem in action. Run the corresponding numerical calculation:

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the policy-gradient derivation</summary>
For a finite policy, differentiate $J(\theta)=\sum_a p_\theta(a)Q(a)$ and use $p'_\theta(a)=p_\theta(a)\psi(a)$. This gives $J'(\theta)=\mathbb E[\psi Q]$. Differentiating $\sum_a p_\theta(a)=1$ gives $\mathbb E[\psi]=0$, so subtracting any action-independent baseline leaves the gradient unchanged.
</details>

## Compatible approximation and information geometry

Score features define a second-moment matrix related to [Fisher information](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fisher_information). Inspect its entries and then multiply it by a weight vector.

